# Secret-scanner false positives, reviewed individually.
#
# assets/skills/ is vendored verbatim from databricks/databricks-agent-skills at
# the tag assets/artifacts/manifest.json pins, and its content SHA-256 is
# verified against that manifest at boot. Editing a vendored file to appease a
# scanner would break that digest and make the offline fallback diverge from the
# skills the terminal actually installs, so allowlisting is the only correct fix
# for a placeholder in upstream documentation.

# Documentation placeholder in the Lakebase local-development example: a
# postgres URL whose userinfo, host, and database name are all the literal
# words describing them. No real credential -- the surrounding prose tells the
# reader to keep the real URL in an untracked .env and to use OAuth token
# refresh in production.
#
# Two entries for one finding, because the two hooks fingerprint differently:
# pre-commit runs `gitleaks protect --staged` (path-scoped) and pre-push runs
# `gitleaks detect` (commit-scoped). The commit-scoped entry is tied to the SHA
# that introduced the file and only matters while this branch is unmerged;
# regenerate it with `gitleaks detect --log-opts "-p -1 <sha>"` after a rebase.
